\section{Describe the state transition, not the spelling}

A machine description can answer three different questions about a form: whether its operands and target features allow it, what executing it does, and how to represent it in the output. An assembler can accept a mnemonic without knowing what program effect the selector intends. A selector needs the behavior as well as the operand restrictions. A separate policy can rank legal forms, but timing and resource estimates must not change the meaning of the opcode or relax a constraint.

Blindell's account of Miller's D-MACS provides an early example of separating such knowledge from the expander. The description declares register classes and paths that transfer values between classes or memory; the generated automata guide the emission of transfers until the operands are in permissible locations (\cite[chapter~2, section~2.2.2]{blindell2016instruction}). Blindell also describes Fraser's register transfer lists, which say how an instruction updates observable machine state (\cite[chapter~2, section~2.3.1]{blindell2016instruction}). These describe different aspects of the same target: placing an operand in a suitable register makes a form usable, but it does not establish that the form implements the required operation.

\section{A postincrement load has two results}

Take a toy machine with 32-bit registers and byte-addressed memory. Its optional \texttt{PULL16 d,p} reads two little-endian bytes using old \texttt{p}, zero-extends the resulting word into \texttt{d}, and advances \texttt{p} by two modulo $2^{32}$. Both operands are general registers and must denote different physical registers. The description marks the feature and the distinct-register rule as legality conditions. There are no status flags or device-memory effects in this model. For a mapped access, both updates commit together; for a fault, neither commits. The first failing condition determines the fault: an odd pointer reports alignment before mapping is checked.

Blindell classifies a load with pointer update as \emph{multi-output}: both the data value and the updated pointer are accessible to later instructions. He distinguishes this from disjoint outputs, whose computations do not share inputs, as in independent SIMD lanes (\cite[chapter~1, section~1.2.1]{blindell2016instruction}). Here the old pointer is used twice, for the address and the increment. Even when the IR has no subsequent use for the updated pointer, the architectural write has not disappeared. If old \texttt{p} is still needed after the load, the allocator must keep that value elsewhere or the selector must use a form without writeback.

Treat the load and its updates as one transition with a normal and a fault outcome. In a declarative description, right-hand sides read entry-state values unless an ordering is explicitly specified. Describing the load using the updated pointer would change the access. Describing the pointer write as unconditional would change fault behavior. Describing the register restriction as a scheduling hint would let the allocator generate an unencodable operand pair.

