\section{Test the successful and faulting transitions}

Consider an independent interpreter for this description. A fault report carries the access's base address; the interpreter exposes the register state at the report. Check the feature and distinctness before execution. The remaining guards are runtime checks, not predicates that the compiler must prove before using the instruction to implement a load with the same fault contract.

\begin{verbatim}
require feature(PULL16) and Reg32(d) and Reg32(p) and d != p
a = old.R[p]                      # All address uses read old p.
if a is odd: fault(Alignment, a)
if either byte is unmapped: fault(Unmapped, a)
w = M[a] + 256 * M[a+1]
commit R[d] = w, R[p] = (a+2) mod 2^32
                                  # No write precedes either check.

p=0xFFFFFFFE, bytes=[0x34,0x12] -> d=0x00001234, p=0
p=0x00001000, second byte unmapped -> both registers unchanged
p=0x00001001, unmapped -> Alignment, not Unmapped
\end{verbatim}

The first access uses the last two addresses in memory. Its sign-independent data calculation is $52+256\cdot18=4660$, and the increment wraps to zero. Another useful data test reads bytes \texttt{FF 80} and requires \texttt{0x000080FF}, not \texttt{0xFFFF80FF}. Initialize \texttt{d} to a sentinel for both fault cases. An implementation that commits \texttt{p} early could leave a correct fault report while still corrupting the register state. An attempted \texttt{PULL16 r4,r4} must instead fail before emission, not become a legal instruction with an arbitrary priority between writes.

\section{Use the description at more than one boundary}

\Cref{fig:visual-262} orders the runtime checks for an already legal \texttt{PULL16}. Both fault exits precede the register writes.

