Suppose the baseline implements the IR with an ordinary load followed by a nonfaulting pointer increment. If the load uses the old pointer and faults without writing anything, it is valid to merge the pair when no observation between those instructions is required and the fault outcomes agree. If the original contract requires an intervening observation, or requires the increment before a possibly faulting read, the combined form is not justified by the success equation alone. Read and write sets are useful for finding the candidate; they do not express the conditions under which the writes occur.

At the selector interface, name old \texttt{p} and new \texttt{p} as distinct values, even though the instruction ties them to the same physical location. Record that \texttt{d} must not overlap that location. The allocator can then preserve the old value by inserting a copy when needed, and the emitter can check distinctness again on the assigned operands. If a transformation discards the pointer result, keep the write or clobber in the machine-level form. Forgetting it makes the description look simpler at the expense of later passes that rely on knowing what an instruction destroys.

A practical backend can generate operand checks and semantic tests from one description. Still test at least some cases against an independent interpretation of the architecture: an expander and a checker generated from the same mistaken transition can agree on the wrong answer. Try a disabled feature, tied physical operands, a successful update, and a fault before commit. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-264} uses similarly explicit instruction effects to recognize combined implementations, while keeping semantic equivalence and form recognition as separate checks.
